---
source: crates/safix/src/reporter.rs
expression: render_plain(&refusal)
---
safix: a commit made here would be authored by 'Mallory Example <mallory@example.com>', and
flake.safix.users declares nobody of that name.

flake.safix.users.bob is delegated to flake.safix.organizations.acme by flake.safix.users.bob.managedBy.
A delegated scaffold is judged against the identity its own commit will
carry, so this run stops before editing anything.

That identity is user.name and user.email as this repository resolves them,
because the commit is the act: there is no flag naming somebody else, since a
flag would let the check and the attribution disagree. It is matched to a
declared person by name and by nothing else — no declaration maps a git
identity to a person, and taking one from an address's local part is how the
wrong name ends up in history.

Declared people:
  - alice
  - bob
  - carol

Set the identity this repository commits under to a name the fleet declares,
or declare the person these commits already name:

    git config user.name <name>

These refusals bind the cooperative path and are not authorization. The tree is
the authorization: anyone who can commit can edit these declarations by hand,
evaluation refuses structure rather than people, and no delegation record places
a key in any audience. What they buy is that a scaffold and the identity it is
attributed to cannot disagree.
